evrtsp: survive a connect() that fails synchronously

evrtsp_make_request() queues the request on the connection before it
calls evrtsp_connection_connect(). When connect() fails at once, which
on macOS is what a Local Network denial looks like (EHOSTUNREACH), the
request stays queued while the caller (airplay.c's sequence_continue)
frees it. The deferred session_failure() then runs
evrtsp_connection_free(), which walks the queue into freed memory and
segfaults on the player thread. Seen five times in one night as
"device_start: Error sending GET /info to '...'" followed by SIGSEGV in
evrtsp_connection_free.

Take the request back off the queue on a failed connect, and say why
the connect failed while at it -- the error was otherwise silent.

Upstream (owntone-server master, checked 2026-09-03) has the same code.

--- a/src/evrtsp/rtsp.c
+++ b/src/evrtsp/rtsp.c
@@ -1483,8 +1483,22 @@
 	TAILQ_INSERT_TAIL(&evcon->requests, req, next);
 
 	/* If the connection object is not connected; make it so */
-	if (!evrtsp_connected(evcon))
-		return (evrtsp_connection_connect(evcon));
+	if (!evrtsp_connected(evcon)) {
+		int ret = evrtsp_connection_connect(evcon);
+		if (ret < 0) {
+			/*
+			 * connect() failed synchronously (e.g. EHOSTUNREACH,
+			 * which is what macOS returns when local network
+			 * access is denied). Hand the request back to the
+			 * caller: it frees it on error, and leaving it queued
+			 * here made evrtsp_connection_free() walk a freed
+			 * request later and crash.
+			 */
+			TAILQ_REMOVE(&evcon->requests, req, next);
+			req->evcon = NULL;
+		}
+		return (ret);
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * If it's connected already and we are the first in the queue,
@@ -1810,6 +1824,8 @@
 		}
 #else
 		if (errno != EINPROGRESS) {
+			event_warnx("%s: connect to \"%s:%d\" failed: %s",
+			    __func__, address, port, strerror(errno));
 			goto out;
 		}
 #endif
